# OpenSpec — vanilla spec-driven workflow

[OpenSpec](https://github.com/Fission-AI/OpenSpec) structures a change as a validated proposal before
any code: **explore → propose → validate → apply → archive**. Specs are the source of truth; changes
are deltas against them.

## Quick checklist

- Search existing work first: `openspec spec list --long`, `openspec list`.
- Decide scope: new capability vs modify existing capability.
- Pick a unique change-id: kebab-case, **verb-led** (`add-`, `update-`, `remove-`, `refactor-`).
- Scaffold `proposal.md`, `tasks.md`, `design.md` (only if needed), and one delta spec per affected
  capability.
- **Every change needs at least one delta.** A change with no `specs/<capability>/spec.md` fails
  validation, strict or not. When the work genuinely changes no requirement (a correction, a tooling
  change), do not invent a new requirement to satisfy the tool — express the change as
  `## MODIFIED Requirements` on the requirement it refines, restating it in full with its scenarios.
  A near-duplicate `## ADDED` requirement is the wrong answer: it splits the doctrine.
  - Probed on CLI **1.6.0**: the validator's own error message suggests setting `skip_specs: true` in
    the change's `.openspec.yaml` for a change that touches no specs. **It has no effect** — neither
    `skip_specs` nor `skipSpecs` suppresses the error. Treat the delta as mandatory.
- Validate: `openspec validate <change-id> --strict` — fix until it passes.
- **Do not start implementation until the proposal is approved.**

## When a proposal is required

Create one for: new features/capabilities, breaking changes (API/schema), architecture or pattern
changes, behavior-changing optimizations, security-pattern updates. Skip it for: bug fixes restoring
intended behavior, typos/formatting/comments, non-breaking dependency bumps, config tweaks. Unclear →
propose (safer).

## Lifecycle

| Stage | Command / skill | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| Explore | `/opsx:explore` | Thinking-partner mode: read the codebase, align the requirement — no implementation. |
| Propose | `/opsx:propose <name>` | Creates the change and generates proposal → spec deltas → design → tasks. |
| Validate | `openspec validate <id> --strict` | **Gate before coding.** Checks the delta specs only: the change needs at least one delta, and every requirement in a delta needs at least one `#### Scenario:` — both rejected with or without `--strict`; the flag promotes WARNING-class findings to failures. Headings in `proposal.md`/`design.md`/`tasks.md` are NOT checked in either mode (see *What `--strict` measures*). |
| Apply | `/opsx:apply [<id>]` | Reads proposal/specs/design/tasks, implements each task, marks `[x]`. |
| Archive | `/opsx:archive [<id>]` | Verifies completeness, syncs delta specs into `specs/`, moves the change to `archive/`. |

## Artifact formats

**`proposal.md`** — `# Change: <title>`, `## Why` (1–2 sentences), `## What Changes` (bullets; mark
**BREAKING**), `## Impact` (affected specs + code).

**Spec deltas** — `changes/<id>/specs/<capability>/spec.md`, one file per affected capability:

```markdown
## ADDED Requirements
### Requirement: <name>
The system SHALL ...

#### Scenario: <case>
- **WHEN** ...
- **THEN** ...

## MODIFIED Requirements   (repeat the complete modified requirement)
## REMOVED Requirements    (add **Reason** and **Migration**)
```

Every requirement needs at least one `#### Scenario:` — the CLI rejects a delta without one as an
ERROR in both modes, with or without `--strict` (see *What `--strict` measures*).

**`tasks.md`** — numbered groups of checkboxes (`- [ ] 1.1 ...`). Mark `[x]` immediately after each
task is done and validated, not in a batch at the end.

**`design.md`** — only when the change is cross-cutting, adds a dependency, touches data models,
has security/performance/migration complexity, or has open technical decisions. Skeleton:
`## Context`, `## Goals / Non-Goals`, `## Decisions` (with alternatives considered), `## Risks /
Trade-offs`, `## Migration`.

### What `--strict` measures

The proposal/design skeletons above are conventions the CLI does not read. Probed on OpenSpec CLI
1.6.0 (2026-09-04), each case run twice — `openspec validate probe` and `openspec validate probe
--strict` — on a change whose `proposal.md` was one line of plain text (no `## Why`, no `## What
Changes`, no `## Impact`):

| Delta under `specs/` | Without `--strict` | With `--strict` |
|---|---|---|
| One requirement with a Scenario | `Change 'probe' is valid`, exit 0 | same |
| The Scenario removed | `✗ [ERROR] … ADDED "Probe requirement" must include at least one scenario`, exit 1 | same |
| No `specs/` at all | `✗ [ERROR] file: Change must have at least one delta`, exit 1 | same |

So the delta-format checks — at least one delta, every requirement with at least one
`#### Scenario:` — are ERRORs that fail with or without the flag, and no mode reads the sections of
`proposal.md`, `design.md` or `tasks.md`. What `--strict` adds is the treatment of WARNING-class
findings: in the CLI source (`core/validation/validator.js`, `createReport`) a report is valid when
`errors === 0` and, under `--strict`, also when `warnings === 0`. No probe separated the two modes
on a change: the change path's two WARNING sites did not fire — a `## ADDED Requirements` header
with no requirement under it is rejected as an ERROR before it counts as a delta (`Delta sections
## ADDED Requirements were found, but no requirement entries parsed`, same output in both modes),
and the delta description the other site measures is generated by the parser, not read from the
file. The difference was observed on a main spec: `openspec validate probe-spec --type spec`
with a `## Purpose` of one word → `Specification 'probe-spec' is valid`, exit 0; the same with
`--strict` → `⚠ [WARNING] overview: Purpose section is too brief (less than 50 characters)`, exit 1.
A project that needs the proposal/design/tasks sections enforced adds its own gate (the DriveZone
fork does: `openspec-drivezone`, *The hard gate*).

## Directory layout

```
openspec/
  project.md                # project conventions the AI must follow
  specs/<capability>/spec.md      # current truth (updated only at archive)
  changes/<change-id>/            # active proposal
    proposal.md  design.md  tasks.md  specs/<capability>/spec.md
  changes/archive/<date>-<id>/    # completed changes = history + delta base
```

## Execution rules

- Always run `openspec validate <id> --strict` **before** implementing — it is the gate, not a
  formality.
- Never edit generated `/opsx` skills or `schema.yaml` by hand; they change via the CLI
  (`openspec update`) or a deliberate fork.
- Current specs live in `openspec/specs/`; `archive/` is history and the base for future deltas.

